@packages/core import { SigilCore }

component SigilCompiler {
  goal {
    Give designers and architects a compiler-like improvement loop for high-level Sigil contracts through profile-scoped deterministic and agentic evaluation.

    Orchestrate evidence-backed evaluation without modifying the selected workspace, replacing human design approval, or moving semantic judgment into SigilCore.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationInvocation {
      compile(workspacePath, target, profileName, options?) streams CompilationEvent and returns CompilationReport

      options may disable external history lookup or explicitly export the resulting CompilationReport
    }

    CompilationTarget {
      a component is the primary compilation unit

      a file target resolves to every component represented by that file

      a workspace target resolves to a dependency-aware collection of components

      component evaluation includes matching expands, direct dependency contracts, relevant glossary context, owned implementation targets, and affected importers
    }

    CompilationProfile {
      a named profile selects an ordered dependency graph of stages, evaluators, evaluation skills, capability limits, and execution budgets

      standard and critical-system are built-in profiles

      project profiles may configure or omit evaluation stages

      each run records the exact effective profile and environment fingerprint
    }

    CompilationStatus {
      red means at least one error exists or a required evaluator did not complete

      yellow means no error exists and at least one warning exists

      green means every required evaluator completed with zero errors and zero warnings

      optimization and information messages do not change the compilation color
    }

    CompilationDiagnostic {
      every error and warning has a stable rule code, diagnostic fingerprint, originating stage and skill, primary location, evidence, impact, correction guidance, and evaluator provenance

      diagnostics may identify related locations and new, unchanged, resolved, or regressed lifecycle state
    }

    CompilationReport {
      the versioned JSON CompilationReport is the authoritative durable result

      terminal, Markdown, CI, and editor presentations are projections of the authoritative report

      a report binds its status to source hashes, component graph, effective profile, stage set, evaluator environment, and completed passes

      a relevant source, profile, skill, adapter, or evaluator-environment change makes an earlier report stale
    }

    EvaluationSkillPackage {
      a compiler-native evaluation skill contains SKILL.md guidance and a compile.json manifest

      the manifest declares pass identity, version, inputs, dependencies, required capabilities, diagnostic rules, and output contract
    }

    AgentAdapter {
      an adapter executes a bounded evaluation request through one configured code-agent provider

      an adapter reports its enforceable capabilities before evaluation

      adapter output is untrusted until it satisfies the compiler-owned protocol and evidence requirements
    }

    CompilationEventProtocol {
      CompilationEvent uses a versioned JSON Lines envelope containing protocolVersion, runId, sequence, type, and payload

      a completed terminal event contains the authoritative CompilationReport

      failed and cancelled runs emit terminal events whenever the process remains able to report them
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompiler {
  state {
    CompilationRunLifecycle {
      A compilation run is queued, running, completed, failed, cancelled, or superseded.
    }

    EvaluationStageLifecycle {
      An enabled stage is pending, running, completed, failed, skipped-by-dependency, or cancelled.

      A stage omitted by the effective profile is disabled rather than incomplete.
    }

    DiagnosticLifecycle {
      A diagnostic is new, unchanged, resolved, or regressed relative to the selected prior report.
    }
  }

  logic {
    CompilationTarget {
      Resolve the selected component before gathering its collective Sigil, dependency, glossary, importer, and owned-implementation context.

      Schedule workspace targets in dependency-aware component order.
    }

    CompilationProfile {
      Load built-in defaults and merge the selected namespaced tools.compile project profile.

      Validate the profile before invoking any evaluator.

      Compute the profile fingerprint from effective stages, skills, adapters, models, capabilities, budgets, and relevant versions.
    }

    PipelineExecution {
      Run deterministic foundation stages before dependent agentic stages.

      Run every enabled stage whose dependencies produced trustworthy inputs.

      Skip only stages whose declared dependencies failed or became unavailable.

      Aggregate every trustworthy diagnostic produced by enabled stages.
    }

    AgentAdapter {
      Supply each adapter only the bounded context and capabilities declared for its evaluation request.

      Validate structured adapter output before normalizing any finding.

      Reject an adapter that cannot enforce the effective capability contract.
    }

    CompilationDiagnostic {
      Derive diagnostic identity from stable rule, stage, subject, and source-location facts rather than evaluator prose.

      Reject or reduce unsupported evaluator claims to information so they cannot make compilation red or yellow.

      Compare stable fingerprints with the selected prior report to derive lifecycle state.
    }

    CompilationStatus {
      Produce red when an error exists or a required evaluator fails, is unavailable, or returns invalid output.

      Produce yellow when no error exists and one or more warnings exist.

      Produce a compiler-owned warning when required evaluators materially disagree.

      Produce green only when every required evaluator completes and no error or warning remains.
    }

    CompilationHistory {
      Read and update interactive run history only through the operating-system user cache.

      Import or export durable reports only when explicitly requested.

    }
  }

  constraints {
    DependencyDirection {
      SigilCompiler depends on SigilCore for deterministic workspace semantics.

      SigilCore does not depend on SigilCompiler, evaluation skills, agent adapters, CLI behavior, or editor behavior.
    }

    ReadOnlyCompilation {
      Compilation does not modify Sigil source, implementation source, configuration, tests, documentation, or other selected-workspace content.

      Compilation does not generate code or execute implementation experiments.
    }

    ProfileConfiguration {
      Compiler configuration lives in the tools.compile namespace of ConfigFile.

      SigilCore preserves tools.compile without interpreting its compiler-owned schema.

      Credentials and secret values never appear in tools.compile.
    }

    StageConfiguration {
      Profiles may enable, disable, order, and configure evaluation stages subject to declared stage dependencies.

      A stage intentionally disabled by the effective profile is not required for green.

      The report and profile fingerprint expose every disabled stage.
    }

    BuiltInProfiles {
      standard requires deterministic foundation, semantic readiness, cross-Sigil coherence, architecture and design, and current-code compatibility through one configured evaluator.

      critical-system includes standard behavior and additionally requires standards and risk evaluation, multiple evaluators, and disagreement detection.
    }

    CapabilityPolicy {
      Every profile declares readable repository scope, executable tool categories, network policy, execution budgets, sensitive exclusions, and applicable research requirements.

      Network access is disabled unless the effective profile explicitly enables it.

      Enabled network access uses declared destination restrictions when the adapter supports destination enforcement.

      An adapter capability mismatch fails the required evaluation rather than broadening access.
    }

    EvidenceThreshold {
      An error identifies a violated rule or contradictory contract and provides reproducible deterministic or repository evidence.

      A warning cites concrete evidence and states the remaining ambiguity or material risk.

      An optimization explains its expected benefit and accepted trade-off.

      Hidden reasoning is never required or retained as diagnostic evidence.
    }

    CompilationDiagnostic {
      Evaluation skills may propose findings but cannot redefine compiler severities, diagnostic identity, lifecycle, or compilation color.

      Required evaluator disagreement produces a warning until the conflict is resolved.

      Green permits no warning waiver.
    }

    CompilationReport {
      CompilationReport excludes credentials, hidden reasoning, and complete private prompts.

      Ordinary compilation does not create report or cache files inside the selected workspace.

      Explicit report export is separate from ordinary compilation and does not imply human approval.
    }

    HumanAuthorityBoundary {
      Green status is evidence that one exact source snapshot passed one exact profile.

      Green status does not approve the design, authorize implementation, or replace an accountable human review.
    }

    CompilationEventProtocol {
      JSON Lines mode writes only CompilationEvent envelopes to standard output.

      Human progress and host logs use standard error and never corrupt the structured event stream.

      Event sequence values increase within one run and terminal events end that run's stream.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilerBoundary {
      Decision: Place agent orchestration in a separate @qoherent/sigil-compiler package and expose it through thin host façades.

      Scope: Governs agentic Sigil compilation; excludes deterministic language interpretation owned by SigilCore.

      Trade-offs: Distribution gains another package and compatibility boundary.

      Design issues addressed: Agentic evaluation must not weaken the deterministic core contract.

      Discarded alternatives: Making SigilCore or SigilCli directly own semantic evaluation was rejected.

      Consequences: CLI, editors, and future hosts share one compiler protocol without duplicating orchestration.
    }

    ReadOnlyCompilation {
      Decision: Keep compilation read-only and exclude implementation-feasibility evaluation, code generation, and implementation experiments from compiler responsibility.

      Scope: Governs every compilation stage and adapter.

      Trade-offs: The compiler cannot empirically validate whether a proposed contract can be implemented.

      Consequences: Compilation evaluates contracts and repository evidence while implementation validation remains a downstream team responsibility.
    }

    ProfileScopedCompilation {
      Decision: Compute status against an exact named profile and environment fingerprint whose stages are configurable.

      Scope: Governs built-in and project-defined profiles.

      Trade-offs: Green is never an unconditional property of a Sigil file.

      Consequences: Teams can configure evaluation depth while preserving an auditable statement of what was evaluated.
    }

    CompilationColor {
      Decision: Use red for errors or incomplete required evaluation, yellow for warnings without errors, and green only for complete evaluation with zero errors and zero warnings.

      Scope: Governs compiler, CLI, CI, and editor status presentation.

      Discarded alternatives: Warning waivers that still produce green were rejected.

      Consequences: A green result has one strict meaning across surfaces.
    }

    ProviderNeutralEvaluation {
      Decision: Let the compiler own the evaluation protocol, diagnostic schema, and native skill-package contract while adapters only execute bounded requests.

      Scope: Governs every agent and evaluation skill integration.

      Trade-offs: Provider-specific features require explicit adapter mapping.

      Consequences: Changing providers does not change compiler semantics.
    }

    EvidenceBackedDiagnostics {
      Decision: Allow only evidence-backed errors and warnings to affect compilation color.

      Scope: Governs deterministic, model-assisted, repository, and experimental findings.

      Trade-offs: Some plausible concerns remain informational until sufficient evidence exists.

      Consequences: Unsupported model claims cannot block progress toward green.
    }

    StableDiagnosticLifecycle {
      Decision: Derive stable diagnostic fingerprints from rule, stage, subject, and location facts and compare them across runs.

      Scope: Governs report comparison and editor progress feedback.

      Trade-offs: Rules must define stable semantic subjects independently from message wording.

      Consequences: Users can distinguish new, unchanged, resolved, and regressed findings.
    }

    ExternalCompilationHistory {
      Decision: Keep implicit interactive history outside the repository and make durable report transfer explicit.

      Scope: Governs local cache, CI, and team handoff; excludes explicit user-selected export destinations.

      Consequences: Compilation remains read-only while lifecycle comparison remains available.
    }

    ComponentCenteredCompilation {
      Decision: Make components the primary compilation unit and resolve file and workspace targets into component sets.

      Scope: Governs target resolution across CLI, editor, and CI surfaces.

      Consequences: A component cannot appear green while a matching expand or relevant relationship remains unevaluated.
    }

    HumanAuthorityBoundary {
      Decision: Treat green as profile-scoped evidence rather than design approval or implementation authorization.

      Scope: Governs every compilation report and downstream workflow.

      Consequences: Critical design handoff retains accountable human review.
    }

    CompilationEventProtocol {
      Decision: Use a versioned JSON Lines event stream with one authoritative report in the completed terminal event.

      Scope: Governs streaming between SigilCompiler and CLI, editor, CI, and automation consumers.

      Trade-offs: Consumers must process an ordered stream instead of parsing one final JSON object.

      Design issues addressed: Long-running compilation requires incremental progress without mixing human output with machine-readable data.

      Discarded alternatives: Mixed stdout and one unframed JSON document were rejected because they cannot safely carry incremental events.

      Consequences: Hosts share one stream protocol and can validate ordering, compatibility, completion, failure, and cancellation.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationStatus {
      A syntax error produces red and prevents only stages that require a valid resolved contract.

      Zero errors with one architecture warning produces yellow.

      Complete evaluation with zero errors and zero warnings produces green.

      Remaining optimization messages do not prevent green.
    }

    StageConfiguration {
      A profile with an optional evaluation stage explicitly disabled can become green and records that disabled stage in its fingerprint and report.
    }

    AgentAdapter {
      A required adapter that cannot enforce the configured network policy fails its evaluation and produces red.

      Invalid or unsupported adapter output cannot introduce an error or warning.
    }

    EvaluatorDisagreement {
      Two required evaluators reaching materially incompatible findings produce a compiler warning and yellow status.
    }

    CompilationTarget {
      Compiling one component includes every matching expand even when those expands live in other files.
    }

    CompilationHistory {
      Recompiling after resolving one stable finding marks it resolved in comparison with the prior report.
    }

    CompilationReport {
      Changing relevant Sigil content or the effective profile makes an exported green report stale.

      A green report handed to an implementation team still requires human approval before implementation begins.
    }

    CompilationEventProtocol {
      A JSON Lines run emits ordered progress events followed by one completed event containing CompilationReport.

      A cancelled run emits a cancelled terminal event when the process remains able to write it.
    }
  }
}
